<div class="card p-4 mb-4 shadow-sm" data-aos="fade-left" data-aos-delay="100">
    <div class="d-flex align-items-start mb-4">
        <div class="bg-warning rounded-circle d-flex align-items-center justify-content-center me-3" style="width: 50px; height: 50px;">
            <i class="fas fa-landmark text-white"></i>
        </div>
        <div>
            <h4 class="text-primary mb-2">Traditional/Cultural Certificate</h4>
            <p class="text-muted mb-3">
                The Traditional/Cultural Certificate is an official document issued by the Ministry of Internal Affairs (MIA) in Liberia. It certifies individuals who actively practice traditional activities, such as traditional healing, cultural leadership, zoe (traditional spiritual leadership), or traditional priesthood, under the customary laws and cultural heritage of Liberia. This certificate recognizes the practitioner’s role in preserving and promoting Liberian cultural traditions and may be required for official recognition, participation in cultural governance, or compliance with legal processes involving traditional roles. Issued under the MIA’s jurisdiction, it ensures statutory compliance with Liberian laws governing customary practices, as guided by the Equal Rights of the Customary Marriage Law of 1998 and related regulations.
            </p>
            
            <div class="mb-4">
                <h5 class="text-primary mb-2"><i class="fas fa-list-check me-2"></i>Key Requirements</h5>
                <p class="text-muted mb-2">
                    To obtain a Traditional/Cultural Certificate, you must provide specific documents and evidence to verify your identity, role, and legitimacy as a traditional practitioner. These requirements ensure the MIA can confirm your active engagement in cultural practices and compliance with Liberian customary law.
                </p>
                <ul class="list-unstyled mb-0">
                    <li><i class="fas fa-check text-success me-2"></i><strong>Identification</strong>: A valid birth certificate, national ID card, or passport to confirm your identity.</li>
                    <li><i class="fas fa-check text-success me-2"></i><strong>Payment</strong>: A tax payment receipt from the Liberia Revenue Authority (LRA), required for issuing the certificate. The receipt number is recorded on the document for authenticity.</li>
                    <li><i class="fas fa-check text-success me-2"></i><strong>Notarized Affidavit</strong>: A sworn statement signed before a notary public, attesting to your active role as a traditional healer, cultural leader, zoe, or traditional priest.</li>
                    <li><i class="fas fa-check text-success me-2"></i><strong>Testimonial from Traditional Authority</strong>: A notarized letter or affidavit from a recognized traditional chief, governor, or cultural council verifying your practice and standing in the community.</li>
                    <li><i class="fas fa-check text-success me-2"></i><strong>Evidence of Practice</strong>: Documentation or testimonials (e.g., community letters, records of ceremonies, or client testimonies) demonstrating your ongoing engagement in traditional activities.</li>
                </ul>
            </div>
            
            <div class="mb-4">
                <h5 class="text-primary mb-2"><i class="fas fa-dollar-sign me-2"></i>Fee</h5>
                <p class="text-success fw-bold mb-0">$50.00 USD</p>
                <p class="text-muted mt-1">
                    This fee is paid to the Liberia Revenue Authority (LRA) and covers the cost of processing and issuing the certificate, ensuring fiscal compliance with Liberian regulations.
                </p>
            </div>
        </div>
    </div>
    
    <div class="accordion" id="traditionalCategoriesAccordion">
        <h5 class="text-primary mb-3"><i class="fas fa-tasks me-2"></i>Category and Procedures</h5>
        
        <!-- Standard Traditional/Cultural Practice Accordion Item -->
        <div class="accordion-item">
            <h2 class="accordion-header" id="headingStandardTraditional">
                <button class="accordion-button" type="button" data-bs-toggle="collapse" data-bs-target="#collapseStandardTraditional" aria-expanded="true" aria-controls="collapseStandardTraditional">
                    Standard Traditional/Cultural Practice
                </button>
            </h2>
            <div id="collapseStandardTraditional" class="accordion-collapse collapse show" aria-labelledby="headingStandardTraditional" data-bs-parent="#traditionalCategoriesAccordion">
                <div class="accordion-body">
                    <p>
                        The Standard Traditional/Cultural Practice category applies to individuals actively engaged in recognized traditional roles, such as traditional healers (who provide herbal or spiritual remedies), cultural leaders (who preserve tribal customs and governance), zoe (spiritual leaders in certain Liberian tribes), or traditional priests (who conduct rituals and ceremonies). The Ministry of Internal Affairs (MIA) verifies your role through a rigorous process to ensure you are a legitimate practitioner contributing to Liberia’s cultural heritage. This certificate is essential for official recognition, participation in cultural councils, or legal processes involving traditional authority, as it provides formal proof of your status under Liberian customary law.
                    </p>
                    <h6 class="text-primary mb-2">Steps to Obtain the Certificate</h6>
                    <ul class="list-unstyled">
                        <li>
                            <i class="fas fa-arrow-right text-primary me-2"></i>
                            <strong>Submit a Written Request</strong>: Send a formal request to the MIA’s Legal Bureau, specifying your role (e.g., traditional healer, zoe). While not mandatory, working with a lawyer or law firm is highly recommended to ensure your application is complete and meets legal and cultural standards, making the process smoother.
                        </li>
                        <li>
                            <i class="fas fa-arrow-right text-primary me-2"></i>
                            <strong>Provide Identification</strong>: Submit a valid birth certificate, national ID card, or passport. This document verifies your identity and is a crucial part of the MIA’s record-keeping process for cultural practitioners.
                        </li>
                        <li>
                            <i class="fas fa-arrow-right text-primary me-2"></i>
                            <strong>Submit a Notarized Affidavit</strong>: Provide a sworn statement (affidavit) signed before a notary public, attesting to your active role as a traditional healer, cultural leader, zoe, or traditional priest. This legal attestation confirms your commitment to your practice.
                        </li>
                        <li>
                            <i class="fas fa-arrow-right text-primary me-2"></i>
                            <strong>Obtain a Testimonial from a Traditional Authority</strong>: Secure a notarized letter or affidavit from a recognized traditional chief, governor, or cultural council. This testimonial verifies your legitimacy, standing, and contributions to the community’s cultural practices.
                        </li>
                        <li>
                            <i class="fas fa-arrow-right text-primary me-2"></i>
                            <strong>Provide Evidence of Practice</strong>: Submit documentation or testimonials demonstrating your ongoing engagement in traditional activities. Examples include letters from community members, records of ceremonies you’ve conducted, or testimonies from clients you’ve served (e.g., patients for healers).
                        </li>
                        <li>
                            <i class="fas fa-arrow-right text-primary me-2"></i>
                            <strong>Pay the LRA Tax</strong>: Make a tax payment to the Liberia Revenue Authority (LRA). The receipt number is recorded on the certificate, serving as proof of fiscal compliance with Liberian regulations and ensuring the document’s authenticity.
                        </li>
                        <li>
                            <i class="fas fa-arrow-right text-primary me-2"></i>
                            <strong>MIA Verification and Issuance</strong>: The MIA conducts due diligence to confirm your role and legitimacy as a traditional practitioner. This involves verifying your documents, consulting with traditional authorities, and ensuring statutory compliance with Liberian customary law. Once verified, the MIA issues the certificate, which includes the LRA receipt number for legal validity.
                        </li>
                    </ul>
                </div>
            </div>
        </div>
    </div>
</div>